Make the boot report progress through a channel that cannot die (judyp bring-up).

Three instrumentation rounds in a row came back with a photograph identical field for field, and the
reason is now known from the source, not from guessing:

  1. printf/IOLog die at PE_init_printf(TRUE): PE_init_iokit's first statement points PE_putc at the
     video console, and the panel console never shows a line printed after kdp_serial_init.
  2. The df373d8 breadcrumbs called boot_progress_stage, which lives in the payload
     (package/BootProgress.c:807), not in the kernel. The CI stub archive -- built for exactly the
     probe symbols that boot progress imports -- satisfied the kernel's reference with an empty
     no-op, so every call vanished at link time. BC00 could never have appeared.

Static reading also narrows the stall: PE_init_iokit's second statement is printf("iBoot version"),
and that line has never appeared on the panel console, which stays alive up to the kdp stage. So the
kernel stops inside PE_init_printf(TRUE) -- the console/screen takeover -- before InitIOKit and
before anything IOKit-related. The three earlier rounds instrumented code that never executed.

This patch therefore instruments through the one channel that cannot die: the panel's MEM row prints
vm_page_wire_count, read live from kernel memory by the payload itself. Entry to PE_init_iokit adds
256 pages (identity: 56516 -> 56772, far outside natural build-to-build spread), and each checkpoint
passed adds 4, so the photograph reads wired = 56516 + 256 + 4*N and N names the last completed
step (table in pexpert/arm/pe_init.c):

  0 entered PE_init_iokit   1 PE_init_printf returned   2 first printf completed
  3 pe_prepare_images       4 before InitIOKit          5-17 InitIOKit internals
 18 InitIOKit returned     19 ConfigureIOKit returned   20 lockdown entered
 21 zalloc_iokit_lockdown  22-25 matching/workLoop/registerService   26 StartIOKitMatching returned

If wired stays 56516 while the stage row still shows PE_init_iokit, the payload on the device is the
old kernel and the problem is the flash chain, not the code. The kheap_alloc change to
OSObject_typed_operator_new from the previous round is gone: upstream runs this XNU on the same SoC
without touching the allocator, and the stall is now known to be before InitIOKit anyway.

R-DT1 (2026-11-21): the macro calls boot_progress_stage() again next to the wire bump. The 14:28
photograph did show BC13..BC18, and llvm-nm finds _boot_progress_stage defined in the kernel image
(0xfffffff00bfa0688), so the "stub ate the calls" reading above was wrong; restoring the text keeps
the old chain visible in the same frame as the new BC50..BC56 points in IODeviceTreeAlloc.

diff --git a/iokit/Kernel/IOPlatformExpert.cpp b/iokit/Kernel/IOPlatformExpert.cpp
index d0149a6..372aaf9 100644
--- a/iokit/Kernel/IOPlatformExpert.cpp
+++ b/iokit/Kernel/IOPlatformExpert.cpp
@@ -2086,15 +2086,25 @@ IOPlatformExpertDevice::init(void *dtRoot)
 	return true;
 }
 
+/* UNI_CK: see the comment in iokit/Kernel/IOStartIOKit.cpp -- every site bumps vm_page_wire_count
+ * by 4; BC30..BC33 are N=22..25 in the execution-order table in pexpert/arm/pe_init.c. */
+extern "C" unsigned int vm_page_wire_count;
+extern "C" void boot_progress_stage(const char *text) __attribute__((weak_import));
+#define UNI_BC(text) do { if (boot_progress_stage) boot_progress_stage(text); vm_page_wire_count += 4; } while (0)
+
 bool
 IOPlatformExpertDevice::startIOServiceMatching(void)
 {
+	UNI_BC("BC30 startIOServiceMatching enter");
 	workLoop = IOWorkLoop::workLoop();
+	UNI_BC("BC31 workLoop allocated");
 	if (!workLoop) {
 		return false;
 	}
 
+	UNI_BC("BC32 registerService enter");
 	registerService();
+	UNI_BC("BC33 registerService done");
 
 	return true;
 }
